 ## Background
-Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) has been a cornerstone of robotics and computer vision for decades, traditionally relying on feature-based or direct methods [S1, unofficial]. The advent of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) introduced implicit neural representations for 3D reconstruction, leading to "Neural SLAM" methods that optimize neural networks for both pose and scene representation [S2]. More recently, 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) has emerged as a highly efficient explicit representation, enabling real-time rendering and optimization. This has spurred the development of 3DGS-based SLAM systems, which leverage the differentiable rasterization of Gaussians to achieve faster convergence and higher fidelity than NeRF-based counterparts [S1, unofficial]. Key developments include the integration of uncertainty maps for robust densification [S1, unofficial], multi-agent cooperative mapping using neural point representations [S2], and active mapping strategies that select informative views to maximize reconstruction quality [S3, unofficial][S5].
+The integration of SLAM with 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a natural extension of the original 3DGS paper, which provided a fast, differentiable rendering pipeline but assumed known camera poses. Early works like [S1, unofficial] demonstrated that 3DGS could be used for online SLAM, leveraging its explicit representation for efficient densification. Concurrently, hybrid neural field methods (NeRF-based SLAM) such as [S2] explored implicit representations for robustness. The field has since branched into specialized areas including multi-agent SLAM [S2], active mapping [S3, unofficial][S5], and robust tracking under lighting changes [S4]. The shift from implicit to explicit representations in SLAM aims to combine the robustness of neural fields with the real-time rendering capabilities of Gaussian Splatting.
 
